title: Avoid duplicate fetches on the experimental collator protocol
doc:
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    Follow-up to #11967. Two changes to the validator side of the experimental collator
    protocol:

    **Avoid duplicate fetches for the same advertised collation.**

    Previously, when two peers happened to advertise the same V2 candidate, the planner could
    launch fetches in parallel from both. The fetch dedup compared whole `Advertisement`
    values, which conflated the offer (relay parent, para id, prospective candidate) with
    the carrier `peer_id` — so the same offer carried by two peers produced two distinct
    keys. The duplicate fetch wasted a CQ slot and bandwidth, and the second-completing
    fetch overwrote the first in `fetched_collations`, so slashing attribution to
    a peer depended on which response happened to arrive last.

    The fix splits the type:

    * `Advertisement` is the peer-agnostic identifier of an advertised collation (the on-wire
      identity).
    * `PeerAdvertisement` is `Advertisement` paired with the carrier `peer_id` and the
      carrier-asserted `advertised_descriptor_version`. It's what flows through accept-time
      and fetch-time code wherever the carrier matters (rate-limit, reputation arbitration,
      slashing).

    `advertised_descriptor_version` lives on `PeerAdvertisement` rather than `Advertisement`
    on purpose: V1/V2 protocol messages have no descriptor-version field (decoded as `None`),
    while V3 protocol messages carry it explicitly (`Some(_)`). If the field were part of
    `Advertisement`'s identity, the same candidate hash advertised by a V2-protocol peer and
    a V3-protocol peer would hash to two distinct in-flight keys, letting an attacker
    controlling peers on both protocol versions bypass the dedup and double-fetch the same
    candidate, wasting a CQ slot. Hoisting the field onto the carrier-attached type keeps
    the dedup peer-agnostic while preserving the validation that the fetched descriptor
    version matches what the carrier advertised.

    `PendingRequests` is now keyed by `Advertisement`, with the peer we're fetching from
    stored as the value. "Is a fetch already in flight for this advertisement?" is an O(1)
    lookup. The fetch planner's reputation arbitration picks the rep-best carrier among
    co-advertisers, so a contested advertisement always goes to the highest-reputation
    peer, and that peer is the only one slashed/credited for the resulting candidate.

    Dedup of duplicate advertisements happens at fetch time, not accept time:
    `try_accept_advertisement` parks every advertisement (subject to the per-peer
    rate-limit counter, which counts every attempt regardless), and the fetch planner
    filters out anything already in flight or already fetched via `PendingRequests::contains`
    and `fetched_collations.contains_key`. Co-advertisers' parked ads are useful fallbacks
    if the in-flight fetch fails (peer goes offline, times out, etc.) — we retry from a
    parked co-advertiser without waiting for re-advertisement.

    **Drop `MAX_FETCH_DELAY` (300ms initial fetch delay).** The mechanism does
    not deliver what it was meant to:

    * For V3 (low-latency design) and V2 with `RelayParentOffset > 0` (already in
      production on AssetHub-Westend with `RELAY_PARENT_OFFSET = 1`), the delay's
      anchor (`activated_at` for the scheduling parent) is at least one slot before honest
      collators are ready to advertise. The 300ms window has elapsed long before any
      ad-arbitration could happen.
    * For V2 with `RelayParentOffset = 0`, the window does sit roughly around slot start,
      but only protects collations that finish in under 300ms; for any longer build the
      delay strictly hurts (the rep-0 attacker's fetch starts at t=300ms and runs until
      t=2.3s, blocking the honest ad that arrives at t=2s — without the delay it would
      have started at t=0 and timed out at t=2s, freeing the slot exactly when the honest
      ad arrives).
    * An adaptive attacker can wait until the moment honest collators advertise, making
      the delay break-even at best.

    The protection the delay was approximating — letting honest, lower-reputation peers
    compete with adversarial advertisements — is what parallel fetching
    ([#11023](https://github.com/paritytech/polkadot-sdk/issues/11023)) is designed to
    deliver.

    Test coverage in `validator_side_experimental/tests.rs`:
    * `v2_same_candidate_from_multiple_peers_fetched_once` — two peers carry the same
      offer, exactly one fetch is launched.
    * `v2_co_carrier_rep_arbitration_picks_high_rep_peer` — when two peers carry the same
      offer with different reputations, the fetch goes to the rep-best peer.
    * `cross_protocol_version_carriers_fetched_once` — V2-protocol carrier (`None`) and
      V3-protocol carrier (`Some(_)`) advertising the same candidate dedup to one fetch.

    No public API changes; behavior change is contained to the experimental validator side.
crates:
- name: polkadot-collator-protocol
  bump: patch
